

Processes Management 2 in Linux (Debian based) - follow along video from Linux For Hackers

REFRESH
- to see what's running currently in the terminal
      - ps
- to see running processes for a user, add "-u" switch
      - ps -u "username"
- to filter results for a user, "|" to "grep"
      - ps -u "username" | grep "term"


KILL COMMAND
- general syntax, kill" command needs a process or job ID, which can be found using "ps" command
      - kill -"signal number" "job number or PID"
      - kill "ID"
- there's more than one way to "kill" a process, default is no.15 SIGTERM - this tells the process "please die" and the process might refuse
- the ultimate KILL signal is 9) SIGKILL
- shortcut to find process or job ID
      - pgrep "term"
- a way to KILL a process without using an ID
      - pkill "signal number" "process name, eg. ping"


MONITORING PROCESSES
- shows running processes on Linux and sorts them in order of CPU usage
      - top
- a fancier version
      - htop


FOREGROUND & BACKGROUND
- example using "ping" command, "ping" checks if a website is awake and is a foreground process, "-c" stands for count
      - ping -c "occurence" "url"
- nothing can be done while process is running - use the following hotkeys to KILL 
      - ctl+c
            - this command uses Signal 2) SIGINT (Interupt)
- to put "ping" to sleep (or stop) - this command uses Kill command 19) SIGSTOP - status can be seen in terminal once command given 
      - ctl+z
            - inputing this command again continues the process - uses Signal 18) SIGCONT
- to see any jobs going, type the below - this command shows the job number, status and command initiated
      - jobs
- to move jobs to the background - processes can't be interacted with eg. "ctl+c"
      - bg
- to move specific jobs
      - bg "job number"
- to move it back to the foreground
      - fg "job number"
- make a process go straight to the background
      - ping -c "occurence" "url" &
